QB.add(18,[{"n":1,"q":"A 45-year-old man comes with recurrent episodes of loss of consciousness, tremors, excessive sweating, and confusion. A tumour of the pancreas is suspected, and the serum proinsulin level (on a 72-hour fasting test) is raised. Which of the following investigations would be most preferred?","o":[{"t":"Computed tomography"},{"t":"MRI"},{"t":"Manual Palpation and Intraoperative Ultrasound"},{"t":"Endoscopic Ultrasound (EUS)"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Endoscopic Ultrasound (EUS)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario points to insulinoma for which the main investigation of choice to confirm the diagnosis is endoscopic ultrasound ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Diagnosis of Insulinoma:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Presence of Whipple’s triad:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypoglycemia (plasma glucose < 50 mg/dL)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuroglycopenic symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Relief of symptoms upon glucose administration."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Biochemical diagnosis is made by the following:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma glucose: low."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin/glucose ratio: raised."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C-peptide: raised."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proinsulin during a 72-hour fasting test : raised."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sulfonylurea (metabolites): absence in urine and plasma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Invasive Imaging Techniques for Insulinoma Invasive Diagnostic Modalities for Insulinoma Localization"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Transabdominal ultrasonography Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) CT (Option A) Considered the primary diagnostic tool . MRI (Option B) EUS-guided Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) Somatostatin scintigraphy Angiography with Arterial Secretagogue Venous"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Sampling (ASVS) Manual Palpation and Intraoperative Ultrasound (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe hypoglycemia can be challenging to manage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medications: Diazoxide is historically used to inhibit insulin release but may cause sodium retention and nausea. It is used when preop or intraop localisation is not required. Everolimus is effective in improving glycemic control and may induce insulin resistance and have an antitumor effect. Somatostatin analogues like octreotide normalize blood glucose levels but need to be carefully used as these may worsen hypoglycemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diazoxide is historically used to inhibit insulin release but may cause sodium retention and nausea. It is used when preop or intraop localisation is not required."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Everolimus is effective in improving glycemic control and may induce insulin resistance and have an antitumor effect."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin analogues like octreotide normalize blood glucose levels but need to be carefully used as these may worsen hypoglycemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In high-risk patients, the following may also be done: Alcohol ablation Radiofrequency embolization Embolization"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcohol ablation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiofrequency embolization"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Embolization"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diazoxide is historically used to inhibit insulin release but may cause sodium retention and nausea. It is used when preop or intraop localisation is not required."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Everolimus is effective in improving glycemic control and may induce insulin resistance and have an antitumor effect."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin analogues like octreotide normalize blood glucose levels but need to be carefully used as these may worsen hypoglycemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcohol ablation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiofrequency embolization"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Embolization"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Surgical treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surgical resection is recommended in the absence of metastatic disease: Enucleation is done for exophytic or peripheral insulinomas. Pancreatoduodenectomy for tumours in the head of the pancreas. Distal pancreatectomy with spleen preservation for smaller tumours not involving splenic vessels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Enucleation is done for exophytic or peripheral insulinomas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatoduodenectomy for tumours in the head of the pancreas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Distal pancreatectomy with spleen preservation for smaller tumours not involving splenic vessels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Enucleation is done for exophytic or peripheral insulinomas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatoduodenectomy for tumours in the head of the pancreas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Distal pancreatectomy with spleen preservation for smaller tumours not involving splenic vessels."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 48-year-old woman presents with recurrent episodes of diarrhoea, weight loss, and intermittent abdominal pain over the past 6 months. She also reports occasional flushing and has developed diabetes despite having no previous history of it. On examination, she is noted to have steatorrhea and a mild skin rash. Blood tests reveal elevated fasting plasma glucose levels. Which of the following diagnostic methods is not used in the diagnosis of this patient’s condition?","o":[{"t":"Elevated fasting plasma somatostatin levels"},{"t":"Secretin stimulation test"},{"t":"Somatostatin receptor imaging"},{"t":"Biopsy and immunohistochemistry for somatostatin"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Somatostatin receptor imaging"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient presents with classic symptoms of somatostatinoma , including diarrhoea , weight loss , diabetes , steatorrhea , and flushing , which are commonly associated with somatostatin hypersecretion ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin receptor imaging is primarily used to detect distant metastases of somatostatinoma, not to establish the diagnosis itself. It’s a tool for assessing tumour spread rather than for initial diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of Somatostatinoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Requires clinical signs/symptoms and elevated fasting plasma somatostatin levels (> 25 pmol/L, > 60 pg/mL). (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If results are indeterminate, secretin or calcium stimulation tests may be used (not standardized). (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imaging methods (3-phase CT, MRI, endoscopic ultrasound) assist in localization."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin receptor imaging detects distant metastases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confirm diagnosis through biopsy and immunohistochemistry for somatostatin. (Option D ruled out)"}]},{"n":3,"q":"Assertion: In diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), potassium levels can appear normal or even elevated initially despite a total body potassium deficiency. Reason: Potassium shifts from the intracellular to the extracellular space due to insulin deficiency and acidosis, temporarily raising serum potassium levels.","o":[{"t":"Both assertion and reason are correct, and the reason correctly explains the assertion."},{"t":"Both assertion and reason are correct, but the reason does not correctly explain the assertion."},{"t":"The assertion is correct, but the reason is incorrect."},{"t":"The assertion is incorrect, but the reason is correct."}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Both assertion and reason are correct, and the reason correctly explains the assertion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Assertion : In DKA , the serum potassium may appear normal or elevated initially, despite a total body potassium deficiency . This is because the potassium is shifting out of cells into the extracellular space due to acidosis and insulin deficiency ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reason : I nsulin deficiency and the acidotic state in DKA cause potassium to move from the intracellular space to the extracellular space (including the bloodstream), temporarily increasing serum potassium levels. However, total body potassium is still depleted due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting , which leads to a need for potassium correction during treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Serum electrolytes in DKA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Potassium: Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting. Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sodium: Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose. Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 45-year-old male with type 1 diabetes presents with increased thirst, frequent urination, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain. On examination, he has dry mucous membranes, tachypnea, and a fruity odour to his breath. His blood pressure is 90/60 mmHg. Labs show blood glucose of 450 mg/dL, bicarbonate of 18 mEq/L, and pH of 7.28. Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":[{"t":"Kussmaul breathing is deep, laboured, and rapid."},{"t":"Fruity odour on the breath is caused by acetone."},{"t":"Dehydration in DKA is associated with dry mucous membranes and poor capillary refill."},{"t":"Fever is always present in DKA and indicates infection."}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Fever is always present in DKA and indicates infection."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's h/o type 1 diabetes, high blood glucose, acidosis, dehydration, fruity breath, and Kussmaul breathing strongly indicate Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA), a serious complication triggered by insulin deficiency leading to ketone buildup and metabolic imbalance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fever is not always present in DKA. Fever may occur if there is an infectious trigger , but it is not a constant or universal finding in all DKA patients. Therefore, the presence or absence of fever does not necessarily indicate an infection in every case of DKA."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Signs and Symptoms of Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms of Hyperglycemia : Polyphagia (increased hunger) Polyuria (frequent urination) Polydipsia (increased thirst) Dehydration : (Option C ruled out) Decreased urine output Dry mouth Decreased sweating Poor capillary refill Dry mucous membranes Skin turgor loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polyphagia (increased hunger)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polyuria (frequent urination)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polydipsia (increased thirst)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dehydration : (Option C ruled out) Decreased urine output Dry mouth Decreased sweating Poor capillary refill Dry mucous membranes Skin turgor loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased urine output"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dry mouth"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased sweating"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Poor capillary refill"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dry mucous membranes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skin turgor loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other Common Symptoms : Anorexia (loss of appetite) Nausea Vomiting Abdominal pain Weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anorexia (loss of appetite)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nausea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vomiting"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal pain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Possible Infectious Triggers : Fever Cough Urinary symptoms (if an infection is triggering DKA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cough"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary symptoms (if an infection is triggering DKA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurologic Symptoms (if cerebral oedema develops) : Headache Confusion Altered mental status General drowsiness Focal neurologic deficits"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headache"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confusion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Altered mental status"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"General drowsiness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Focal neurologic deficits"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physical Examination Findings : Tachycardia (increased heart rate) Tachypnea (rapid breathing) Fever or hypothermia (if an infection is present) Hypotension (may occur, especially in severe cases) Ill-appearing Kussmaul breathing (deep, labored, and rapid breathing) (Option A ruled out) Fruity odour to breath (due to acetone) (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tachycardia (increased heart rate)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tachypnea (rapid breathing)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever or hypothermia (if an infection is present)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension (may occur, especially in severe cases)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ill-appearing"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kussmaul breathing (deep, labored, and rapid breathing) (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fruity odour to breath (due to acetone) (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medication and Substance Use History : A review of medications and substance use (drugs/alcohol) may be important in the patient's history of triggers of DKA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A review of medications and substance use (drugs/alcohol) may be important in the patient's history of triggers of DKA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polyphagia (increased hunger)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polyuria (frequent urination)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polydipsia (increased thirst)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dehydration : (Option C ruled out) Decreased urine output Dry mouth Decreased sweating Poor capillary refill Dry mucous membranes Skin turgor loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased urine output"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dry mouth"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased sweating"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Poor capillary refill"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dry mucous membranes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skin turgor loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased urine output"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dry mouth"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased sweating"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Poor capillary refill"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dry mucous membranes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skin turgor loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anorexia (loss of appetite)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nausea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vomiting"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal pain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cough"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary symptoms (if an infection is triggering DKA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headache"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confusion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Altered mental status"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"General drowsiness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Focal neurologic deficits"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tachycardia (increased heart rate)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tachypnea (rapid breathing)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever or hypothermia (if an infection is present)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension (may occur, especially in severe cases)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ill-appearing"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kussmaul breathing (deep, labored, and rapid breathing) (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fruity odour to breath (due to acetone) (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A review of medications and substance use (drugs/alcohol) may be important in the patient's history of triggers of DKA."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 23-year-old male with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus presents to the emergency department with nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain. He reports that he has not taken his insulin for the past two days. On examination, he appears dehydrated, with deep and rapid breathing. Laboratory tests reveal high blood glucose, ketonemia, and metabolic acidosis. Which of the following statements about the pathophysiology of the patient’s condition is TRUE?","o":[{"t":"Insulin deficiency leads to increased glucose utilization by the cells."},{"t":"Elevated counterregulatory hormones increase glycogen synthesis, reducing blood glucose levels."},{"t":"Lipolysis increases free fatty acids (FFA) in the liver, leading to ketogenesis."},{"t":"Increased glucose utilization prevents hyperglycemia and osmotic diuresis."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Lipolysis increases free fatty acids (FFA) in the liver, leading to ketogenesis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient's presentation is consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) . The key features supporting this diagnosis include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperglycemia, ketonemia, and metabolic acidosis in the context of insulin non-compliance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deep and rapid breathing (Kussmaul respirations) is a compensatory mechanism for metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dehydration due to osmotic diuresis from hyperglycemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In DKA, absolute or relative insulin deficiency and elevated counterregulatory hormones (e.g., glucagon, cortisol, catecholamines) result in increased lipolysis. This increases the delivery of FFAs to the liver, which are converted into ketone bodies, contributing to ketogenesis and metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"img","v":5242},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology of DKA"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Insulin deficiency leads to increased glucose utilization by the cells. (Option A): In insulin deficiency , glucose utilization is impaired. Insulin is necessary for glucose uptake into cells. Without it, cells cannot effectively take up glucose, leading to hyperglycemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Elevated counterregulatory hormones increase glycogen synthesis, reducing blood glucose levels. (Option B: Counterregulatory hormones (e.g., glucagon, cortisol, epinephrine) promote glycogen breakdown (glycogenolysis) and gluconeogenesis , not glycogen synthesis . This leads to increased blood glucose levels, not a reduction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased glucose utilization prevents hyperglycemia and osmotic diuresis. (Option D) : Increased glucose utilization does not occur in DKA due to insulin deficiency. The lack of insulin leads to hyperglycemia and osmotic diuresis (due to the high glucose levels), which is one of the hallmark features of DKA."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 35-year-old patient presents to the clinic with persistent diarrhoea, which has led to significant dehydration. After evaluation, it is determined that the diarrhoea is secretory. The patient has a history of diabetes, which is uncontrolled. Which of the following statements best describes the management and mechanisms associated with secretory diarrhoea in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Secretory diarrhoea primarily results from the malabsorption of nutrients"},{"t":"The main goal of management is reducing intestinal secretion"},{"t":"Antisecretory agents like Racecadotril and Crofelemer may help reduce fluid loss"},{"t":"Secretory diarrhoea shows a high osmotic gap that is not related to food intake"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Antisecretory agents like Racecadotril and Crofelemer may help reduce fluid loss"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario is of secretory diarrhoea associated with uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, and antisecretory agents like Racecadotril and Crofelemer help reduce fluid loss."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secretory diarrhoea:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secretory diarrhoea results from the excessive fluid secretion in the intestine. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is driven by the active transport of ions (Nan, Cln, HCOnn, Kn) and solutes (mainly glucose) between the intestinal lumen and bloodstream."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is typically painless and is not affected by fasting with a normal faecal osmotic gap ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of secretory diarrhoea"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Overuse of laxatives Chronic alcoholism Drugs and toxins Bile acid diarrhoea Bacterial infections: Cholera, pancreatic cholera Bowel resection, disease, or fistula Partial bowel obstruction or impaction Hormone-producing tumors: Carcinoid syndrome VIPnoma Medullary cancer Thyroid Mastocytosis Gastrinoma Colorectal villous adenoma Addison’s disease Congenital electrolyte absorption defects Diabetes mellitus: Idiopathic diarrhoea with associated steatorrhea is seen in uncontrolled insulin-dependent diabetes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fluid Secretion Mechanism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It involves various ion and solute transporters, as well as the activation of signalling pathways related to cyclic nucleotides and calcium ions (Ca²n)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Key processes include: Activation of chloride (Cln) channels in the enterocyte's apical membrane. This activation enhances fluid secretion. Simultaneously, inhibition of sodium (Nan) transport decreases fluid absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Activation of chloride (Cln) channels in the enterocyte's apical membrane."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This activation enhances fluid secretion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Simultaneously, inhibition of sodium (Nan) transport decreases fluid absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Activation of chloride (Cln) channels in the enterocyte's apical membrane."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This activation enhances fluid secretion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Simultaneously, inhibition of sodium (Nan) transport decreases fluid absorption."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of secretory diarrhoea:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The primary goal in managing diarrheal diseases is to promptly address dehydration . (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid Replacement: In developed countries, intravenous fluid replacement is commonly used in hospitals. In developing countries and non-hospital settings, oral rehydration solutions (ORS) are the standard treatment. While ORS effectively treats dehydration when administered correctly, it does not alter fluid losses, diarrheal output, or the duration of the illness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In developed countries, intravenous fluid replacement is commonly used in hospitals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In developing countries and non-hospital settings, oral rehydration solutions (ORS) are the standard treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While ORS effectively treats dehydration when administered correctly, it does not alter fluid losses, diarrheal output, or the duration of the illness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In developed countries, intravenous fluid replacement is commonly used in hospitals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In developing countries and non-hospital settings, oral rehydration solutions (ORS) are the standard treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While ORS effectively treats dehydration when administered correctly, it does not alter fluid losses, diarrheal output, or the duration of the illness."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic treatment :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The treatments for diarrhoea are categorized as: Antimotility agents slow down bowel movements. Antisecretory agents reduce fluid secretion into the intestines."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antimotility agents slow down bowel movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antisecretory agents reduce fluid secretion into the intestines."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antimotility agents slow down bowel movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antisecretory agents reduce fluid secretion into the intestines."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antisecretory agents :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reducing intestinal fluid secretion has been a less explored approach in antidiarrheal therapies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They are: Bismuth subsalicylate Racecadotril - Prevents the degradation of endogenous opioids (enkephalins), thereby reducing hypersecretion of water and electrolytes into the intestinal lumen. Crofelemer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bismuth subsalicylate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Racecadotril - Prevents the degradation of endogenous opioids (enkephalins), thereby reducing hypersecretion of water and electrolytes into the intestinal lumen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crofelemer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Octreotide therapy is anticipated to enhance overall health and quality of life, ultimately reducing healthcare costs in the long term."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bismuth subsalicylate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Racecadotril - Prevents the degradation of endogenous opioids (enkephalins), thereby reducing hypersecretion of water and electrolytes into the intestinal lumen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crofelemer"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secretory diarrhoea shows a high osmotic gap is not related to food intake (Option D): Secretory diarrhoea typically shows a low osmotic gap (<50 mOsm/kg) because it is primarily driven by the active secretion of electrolytes and fluids into the gut, and not by the osmotic effect of undigested or unabsorbed solutes (which is characteristic of osmotic diarrhoea)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additionally, secretory diarrhoea is not related to food intake ; it persists regardless of whether the patient eats or not."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is in contrast to osmotic diarrhoea , where the diarrhoea typically improves with fasting (because food intake worsens osmotic diarrhoea by increasing solute load in the intestine)."}]},{"n":7,"q":"Which of the following toxin-producing bacteria does a VIPoma mimic?","o":[{"t":"Shigella dysenteriae"},{"t":"Vibrio cholerae"},{"t":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae"},{"t":"Bordetella pertussis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Vibrio cholerae"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A VIPoma secretes vasoactive intestinal peptide, which causes severe watery diarrhoea by mimicking the action of cholera toxin leading to difficulty in its diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A VIPoma is a type of neuroendocrine tumour that produces vasoactive intestinal peptides (VIP)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They are linked to syndromes like: Verner-Morrison syndrome. Pancreatic cholera. WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhoea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Verner-Morrison syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatic cholera."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhoea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Verner-Morrison syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatic cholera."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhoea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"VIP, a 28-amino-acid peptide, mimics cholera toxin effects by: Stimulating chloride secretion in the small intestine. Increasing smooth muscle contractility. Leading to severe diarrhoea."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stimulating chloride secretion in the small intestine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increasing smooth muscle contractility."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leading to severe diarrhoea."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stimulating chloride secretion in the small intestine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increasing smooth muscle contractility."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leading to severe diarrhoea."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Patients typically present with:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe watery diarrhoea leads to hypokalemia and metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flushing episodes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low stomach acid ( hypochlorhydria )."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased glycogen breakdown."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated calcium levels (hypercalcemia)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tumor Characteristics:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically solitary."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Often located in the pancreatic tail."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated plasma VIP levels are common but not definitive for diagnosis, as they can occur in other conditions like: Inflammatory bowel disease. Post-surgery (small bowel resection). Radiation enteritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inflammatory bowel disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post-surgery (small bowel resection)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation enteritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic laxative or diuretic use can complicate diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inflammatory bowel disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post-surgery (small bowel resection)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation enteritis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Critical management includes: Fluid and electrolyte replacement to address dehydration, hypokalemia, and electrolyte losses."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid and electrolyte replacement to address dehydration, hypokalemia, and electrolyte losses."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Long-term treatment options: Somatostatin analogues are effective in controlling diarrhoea. Surgical resection is recommended for localized tumours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin analogues are effective in controlling diarrhoea."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surgical resection is recommended for localized tumours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid and electrolyte replacement to address dehydration, hypokalemia, and electrolyte losses."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin analogues are effective in controlling diarrhoea."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surgical resection is recommended for localized tumours."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Shigella dysenteriae (Option A) produces the Shiga toxin, which causes not only profuse and often bloody diarrhoea but also abdominal pain , vomiting , fever , and fatigue hence differentiated from VIPoma diarrhoea."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae (Option C) produces the diphtheria toxin that causes respiratory infections and cutaneous ulcers."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bordetella pertussis (Option D) produces two toxins, the pertussis toxin and adenylate cyclase toxin, that cause tracheobronchitis."}]},{"n":8,"q":"Identify the true statement about the condition where the patient presents with high plasma somatostatin levels, steatorrhea, hypochlorhydria, and diabetes mellitus due to a tumour.","o":[{"t":"The tumour primarily occurs in the stomach"},{"t":"This is usually a small tumour that often goes unnoticed"},{"t":"This is rarely associated with MEN 1 syndrome"},{"t":"Metastasis is rare and is treated using chemotherapy for localized disease"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) This is rarely associated with MEN 1 syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis here is somatostatinoma, which is a somatostatin-secreting tumour usually found in the pancreas or duodenum, and it is rarely associated with MEN1 syndrome ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Somatostatinoma is a neuroendocrine tumour that secretes the hormone somatostatin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical presentation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common symptoms include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes mellitus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gallbladder disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypochlorhydria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diarrhea and steatorrhea (fatty stools)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Location and Characteristics:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primarily occurs in the pancreas or duodenum . (vs Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Produces somatostatin that inhibits insulin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically large. (vs Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Often metastatic at the time of diagnosis. (vs Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rarely associated with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN 1)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confirmed by elevated plasma somatostatin levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The use of somatostatin analogues for treatment is debated due to elevated levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surgical intervention is recommended for localized disease. (vs Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Somatostatinoma Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secretion of somatostatin leads to: Symptoms such as steatorrhea, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, anaemia, and weight loss. Hyperglycemia (high blood sugar). Cholelithiasis (gallstones). Low gastric acid output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms such as steatorrhea, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, anaemia, and weight loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperglycemia (high blood sugar)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cholelithiasis (gallstones)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low gastric acid output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features of somatostatinoma syndrome are less common in patients with MEN 1."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms such as steatorrhea, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, anaemia, and weight loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperglycemia (high blood sugar)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cholelithiasis (gallstones)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low gastric acid output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Requires clinical signs/symptoms and elevated fasting plasma somatostatin levels (> 25 pmol/L, > 60 pg/mL)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If results are indeterminate, secretin or calcium stimulation tests may be used (not standardized)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imaging methods (3-phase CT, MRI, endoscopic ultrasound) assist in localization."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin receptor imaging detects distant metastases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confirm diagnosis through biopsy and immunohistochemistry for somatostatin."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 54-year-old female presents with significant weight loss and a characteristic rash as in the image. Blood tests reveal elevated plasma glucagon levels exceeding 1000 pg/mL. A CT scan shows a large mass in the tail of the pancreas. Which of the following statements regarding her condition is correct?","o":[{"t":"The mass is commonly found in the head of the pancreas"},{"t":"The initial treatment includes antibiotics and steroids"},{"t":"The rash seen is necrolytic migratory erythema"},{"t":"Surgical resection is the treatment option for this condition"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) The rash seen is necrolytic migratory erythema"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The scenario here is of the diagnosis glucagonoma, which is characterized by elevated plasma glucagon levels exceeding 1000 pg/mL and the typical rash found in this condition called necrolytic migratory erythema."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glucagonoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glucagonomas are neuroendocrine tumours that arise from multipotential stem cells of endodermal origin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Characteristic dermatitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It presents as necrolytic migratory erythema , primarily affecting intertriginous areas such as the groin and buttocks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The rash may fluctuate in severity (wax and wane)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Other common symptoms:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucose intolerance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Significant weight loss."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tumor Characteristics:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most glucagonomas are large at the time of diagnosis and are typically located in the tail of the pancreas . (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is confirmed by elevated plasma glucagon levels , typically exceeding 1000 pg/mL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT Scan: The initial localization method is a helical multiphasic contrast-enhanced CT scan. High sensitivity for detecting intrapancreatic neuroendocrine tumours, exceeding 80%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The initial localization method is a helical multiphasic contrast-enhanced CT scan."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High sensitivity for detecting intrapancreatic neuroendocrine tumours, exceeding 80%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI: Used for indeterminate lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Used for indeterminate lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The initial localization method is a helical multiphasic contrast-enhanced CT scan."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High sensitivity for detecting intrapancreatic neuroendocrine tumours, exceeding 80%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Used for indeterminate lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"May provide superior sensitivity for identifying liver metastases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin Receptor Scintigraphy (SRS): Positive results are seen in 97% of patients with glucagonoma. It effectively detects small, hidden metastases both within and outside the abdomen. Useful for staging the tumour."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive results are seen in 97% of patients with glucagonoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It effectively detects small, hidden metastases both within and outside the abdomen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Useful for staging the tumour."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive results are seen in 97% of patients with glucagonoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It effectively detects small, hidden metastases both within and outside the abdomen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Useful for staging the tumour."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Initial Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin analogues like octreotide and lanreotide effectively reduce symptoms and manage the rash related to glucagon hypersecretion by inhibiting glucagon. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves total parenteral nutrition and supplementation of amino acids and zinc to address malnutrition and counteract weight loss effects from glucagon."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibiotics, zinc replacement, and steroids may enhance recovery from necrolytic migratory erythema (NME)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Surgical Recommendations:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For localized disease, distal pancreatectomy and splenectomy are advised."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The only curative option for a small percentage of patients with localized tumours. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complications:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Up to 33% of patients may experience a hypercoagulable state, and therefore, perioperative anticoagulation is generally recommended."}],"qi":[5223]},{"n":10,"q":"A 65-year-old female with type 2 diabetes on SGLT2 inhibitors is brought to the emergency department unconscious. Lab results show pH 7.2, HCO3¯ 15 meq/L, RBS 210 mg/dL, and plasma beta-hydroxybutyrate 2.8 mmol/L with normal lactate. Urine analysis reveals the presence of ketones. What condition is most consistent with these findings?","o":[{"t":"Lactic Acidosis"},{"t":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Euglycemic Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar State"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Euglycemic Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Euglycemic Diabetic Ketoacidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Volume depletion from reduced oral intake, vomiting, and osmotic diuresis from glucosuria worsens lipolysis and ketogenesis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"RBS turns out to be <250 mg/dL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SGLT2 inhibitors cause glycosuria and acidemia by ketogenesis, causing near-normal or less abnormally elevated glucose levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic parameters in an unconscious diabetic patient:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Parameters Diabetic Ketoacidosi Lactic Acidosi Hyperglycemic Euglycemic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"s (DKA) (Option B) s (Option A) Hyperosmolar DKA (Option"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Coma (HHS) C) (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"pH (7.35-7.45) 6.8 - 7.3 £7.35 >7.3 6.8 - 7.3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HCO3¯ (22 to 26 <18 <18 >18 <18 meq/L)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ketostix ++++ - +/- ++++"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Plasma beta-hydrox >2.5 Normal <1 >2.5 ybutyrate"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"RBS (mg/dL) 250 - 600 <250 600 - 1200 <200 - 250 Parameters Diabetic Ketoacidosi Lactic Acidosi Hyperglycemic Euglycemic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"s (DKA) (Option B) s (Option A) Hyperosmolar DKA (Option"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Coma (HHS) C) (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Plasma Osmolality 300 - 320 Normal 330 - 380 ~300 (275 to 295 mOsm/kg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ketostix: 3 ketones in DKA: ß-hydroxybutyrate: Not detected by ketostix Acetone (fruity odour of breath) Acetoacetate: Detected by ketosis detection reagent (nitroprusside) Converted into acetone. Can be positive in DKA, Penicillamine and captopril usage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ß-hydroxybutyrate: Not detected by ketostix"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acetone (fruity odour of breath)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acetoacetate: Detected by ketosis detection reagent (nitroprusside) Converted into acetone. Can be positive in DKA, Penicillamine and captopril usage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Converted into acetone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can be positive in DKA, Penicillamine and captopril usage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lactic acidosis: Plasma lactate level increased. Types: Type A: Due to hypoperfusion and hypoxia. Type B: Due to drug (metformin, epinephrine) or toxin interference of cellular metabolism or a nutritional deficiency state, such as thiamine deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma lactate level increased."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types: Type A: Due to hypoperfusion and hypoxia. Type B: Due to drug (metformin, epinephrine) or toxin interference of cellular metabolism or a nutritional deficiency state, such as thiamine deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type A: Due to hypoperfusion and hypoxia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type B: Due to drug (metformin, epinephrine) or toxin interference of cellular metabolism or a nutritional deficiency state, such as thiamine deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperosmolar Coma: Causes severe fluid depletion and reduced cerebral perfusion. Can present with altered mental status and coma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes severe fluid depletion and reduced cerebral perfusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can present with altered mental status and coma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ß-hydroxybutyrate: Not detected by ketostix"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acetone (fruity odour of breath)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acetoacetate: Detected by ketosis detection reagent (nitroprusside) Converted into acetone. Can be positive in DKA, Penicillamine and captopril usage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Converted into acetone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can be positive in DKA, Penicillamine and captopril usage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Converted into acetone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can be positive in DKA, Penicillamine and captopril usage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma lactate level increased."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types: Type A: Due to hypoperfusion and hypoxia. Type B: Due to drug (metformin, epinephrine) or toxin interference of cellular metabolism or a nutritional deficiency state, such as thiamine deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type A: Due to hypoperfusion and hypoxia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type B: Due to drug (metformin, epinephrine) or toxin interference of cellular metabolism or a nutritional deficiency state, such as thiamine deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type A: Due to hypoperfusion and hypoxia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type B: Due to drug (metformin, epinephrine) or toxin interference of cellular metabolism or a nutritional deficiency state, such as thiamine deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes severe fluid depletion and reduced cerebral perfusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can present with altered mental status and coma."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 40-year-old male comes to the clinic with recurrent episodes of hypoglycemia and loss of consciousness. On further evaluation, he is found to have a tumour on the pancreas. Which of the following is not a component of Whipple’s triad to suspect insulinoma?","o":[{"t":"Symptoms of hypoglycemia"},{"t":"Low plasma glucose concentration"},{"t":"Subsequent improvement of symptoms with the use of glucose treatment"},{"t":"Elevated fasting insulin levels"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Elevated fasting insulin levels"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis of insulinoma is suspected when Whipple’s triad is observed in the patient, which does not include elevated fasting insulin levels . Elevated fasting insulin levels, however, help confirm the diagnosis of insulinoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Insulinoma:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Insulinomas are the most common functional pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours (NETs)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Commonly present with hypoglycemic symptoms , which may include: Confusion Headache Disorientation Visual disturbances Irrational behavior Coma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confusion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headache"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Disorientation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Visual disturbances"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irrational behavior"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms may be misdiagnosed as psychiatric conditions initially."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulinoma is suspected when Whipple’s triad is observed in a patient, i.e., Symptoms of hypoglycemia (Option A) A documented low plasma glucose concentration (Option B) and Subsequent improvement of the symptoms with the use of glucose treatment (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms of hypoglycemia (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A documented low plasma glucose concentration (Option B) and"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subsequent improvement of the symptoms with the use of glucose treatment (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confusion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headache"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Disorientation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Visual disturbances"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irrational behavior"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms of hypoglycemia (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A documented low plasma glucose concentration (Option B) and"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subsequent improvement of the symptoms with the use of glucose treatment (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is confirmed by: Elevated fasting insulin levels Increased proinsulin and C-peptide levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated fasting insulin levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased proinsulin and C-peptide levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surreptitious use of insulin or hypoglycemic agents may complicate diagnosis: This confusion is avoided by evaluating proinsulin and C-peptide levels, which should be normal with exogenous insulin use. Measurement of sulfonylurea levels can assist in differentiation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This confusion is avoided by evaluating proinsulin and C-peptide levels, which should be normal with exogenous insulin use."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measurement of sulfonylurea levels can assist in differentiation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated fasting insulin levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased proinsulin and C-peptide levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This confusion is avoided by evaluating proinsulin and C-peptide levels, which should be normal with exogenous insulin use."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measurement of sulfonylurea levels can assist in differentiation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Differential diagnoses:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe liver disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcoholism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Poor nutrition"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Postprandial hypoglycemia (especially after gastric bypass surgery)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulinamatosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nesidioblastosis"}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 45-year-old diabetic woman is brought to the emergency department with complaints of confusion, fatigue, and dizziness. On examination, she has palpitations, sweating, and tremors. Her blood glucose concentration is found to be 48mg/dl. Which of the following is not a common cause of hypoglycemia in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Insulin"},{"t":"Sulfonylureas"},{"t":"Metformin"},{"t":"Meglitinides"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Metformin"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The given clinical scenario shows that the patient is diabetic, and in diabetic patients, the most common cause of hypoglycemia is antidiabetic medications like insulin and sulfonylureas ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Metformin is not typically associated with hypoglycemia. It works by improving insulin sensitivity and does not increase insulin secretion, so it does not cause hypoglycemia on its own."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypoglycemia is defined as a plasma glucose concentration of less than 70mg/dl."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The symptoms of hypoglycemia, however, occur only when the plasma glucose concentration is below 55mg/dl."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Manifestations of Hypoglycemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neuroglycopenic symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"These symptoms result from insufficient glucose supply to the central nervous system. They are: Behavioural changes Confusion Fatigue Seizures Loss of consciousness Cardiac arrhythmias Severe cases may lead to death Clinical Manifestations of Hypoglycemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neurogenic (Autonomic) symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"These are caused by the body's sympathetic response to hypoglycemia. They are of two types as follows:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Adrenergic symptoms (due to norepinephrine and Cholinergic symptoms (due to possibly epinephrine) acetylcholine)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Palpitations Sweating Tremors Hunger Anxiety Paresthesias"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"These symptoms are nonspecific and require confirmation of low plasma glucose levels for attribution to hypoglycemia. Symptoms should resolve upon raising glucose levels (Whipple’s triad)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common signs: Diaphoresis (sweating) Pallor (pale skin) Increased heart rate and systolic blood pressure, though, may not be evident in individuals with frequent hypoglycemic episodes."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neurological effects: Neuroglycopenic symptoms are often noticeable. Transient focal neurological deficits can occur but permanent deficits are rare."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hypoglycemia levels"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Level 1 (Mild) Blood glucose is <70 mg/dL but 54 mg/dL or higher."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Level 2 (Modera Blood glucose is <54 mg/dL. te)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Level 3 (Severe) The individual is unable to function due to mental or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"physical changes caused by low blood glucose and requires assistance from another person."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of hypoglycemia in non-diabetic patients:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcohol"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Critical illnesses End-stage liver disease Sepsis Renal failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"End-stage liver disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sepsis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Starvation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iatrogenic: surreptitious insulin use"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cortisol deficiency"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Malnourishment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tumors: Insulinoma Non-islet cell tumours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulinoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-islet cell tumours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"End-stage liver disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sepsis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulinoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-islet cell tumours"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypoglycemia in diabetic patients:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Drugs are the most common cause of hypoglycemia ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They are: Insulin (Option A ruled out) Sulfonylureas (Option B ruled out) Meglitinides (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sulfonylureas (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Meglitinides (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sulfonylureas (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Meglitinides (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of hypoglycemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe hypoglycemia is treated using IV dextrose followed by a glucose infusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For conscious patients, readily available carbohydrate sources are given."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonpharmacological treatment involves patient education and lifestyle changes."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 55-year-old man presents with persistent abdominal pain and unintentional weight loss over the past 3 months. He reports no significant past medical history and denies any changes in bowel habits. A CT scan of the abdomen reveals a well-circumscribed mass in the pancreas. Laboratory tests show elevated serum glucose levels but normal levels of other pancreatic hormones. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Insulinoma"},{"t":"Gastrinoma"},{"t":"Non-functioning pancreatic NET"},{"t":"Glucagonoma"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Non-functioning pancreatic NET"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-functioning pancreatic NET does not secrete hormones and often remains asymptomatic until it causes anatomic symptoms like abdominal pain and weight loss. Elevated serum glucose levels suggest glucose intolerance, ruling out insulinoma due to a lack of excessive insulin. Normal levels of other pancreatic hormones further support this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Functioning PNET Non-functioning PNET (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Associated with symptoms due to No hormone secretion. hormone secretion . The majority of pancreatic NETs (about 80%) are The most common are insulinomas . nonfunctional."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Often clinically silent until they cause anatomic symptoms . Typically diagnosed incidentally or when symptoms like abdominal pain or weight loss occur . Usually more advanced when found (metastasized)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Functional pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tumor Hormone Symptoms/signs"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Insulinoma (Option A) Insulin Hypoglycemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastrinoma (Option B) Glucagon Peptic ulcer, diarrhoea, GERD"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Somatostatinoma Somatostatin Diarrhea, diabetes, hypochlorhydria,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cholelithiasis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glucagonoma (Option D) Glucagon Necrolytic migratory erythema,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"diabetes, depression"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"VIPoma Vasoactive intestinal Watery diarrhoea, hypokalemia,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"peptide achlorhydria."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 75-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents to the emergency department with confusion and increased thirst over the past week. On examination, he has dry mucous membranes, a weak pulse, and a blood pressure of 85/50 mmHg. Laboratory tests show: (Plasma glucose: 1000 mg/dL, Serum osmolality: 360 mOsm/kg, Serum sodium: 130 mEq/L, Arterial blood gas: pH 7.38, bicarbonate 18 mEq/L). Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Administer IV Ringer Lactate"},{"t":"Administer IV 0.9% normal saline"},{"t":"Start IV insulin bolus of 1 unit/kg with IV Potassium"},{"t":"Administer oral hypoglycemic agents"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Administer IV 0.9% normal saline"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's severe hyperglycemia (1000 mg/dL) and hyperosmolality (360 mOsm/kg), along with signs of dehydration , confirm HHS ( Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state). Initial treatment with IV 0.9% normal saline is essential to restore intravascular volume , stabilize blood pressure,and reduce neurological risks."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laboratory Abnormalities and Diagnosis of HHS"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key Laboratory Features : Marked hyperglycemia (plasma glucose may exceed 55.5 mmol/L [1000 mg/dL]) Hyperosmolality (>350 mOsm/L) Prerenal azotemia Sodium Levels : Measured serum sodium may be normal or slightly low despite hyperglycemia. Corrected serum sodium usually increases (add 1.6 mEq to measured sodium for each 5.6 mmol/L [100 mg/dL] rise in serum glucose). Acidosis : In contrast to DKA, acidosis and ketonemia are typically absent or mild; small anion-gap metabolic acidosis may occur due to increased lactic acid. Ketonuria : Moderate ketonuria, if present, is usually due to starvation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar State (HHS)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fluid Replacement Initial Stabilization : Start with 1–3 litres of 0.9% normal saline over the first 2–3 hours to stabilize hemodynamic status. (Option B) Considerations : HHS involves more significant fluid loss over days to weeks, so the reversal of hyperosmolarity must balance free water repletion and the risk of neurological complications. If serum sodium is >150 mmol/L, use 0.45% saline. Ongoing Fluid Administration : After achieving hemodynamic stability, continue with hypotonic fluids (0.45% saline initially, followed by 5% dextrose in water [D5W]) to correct the free water deficit, which can be as much as 9–10 litres. Administer at a rate of 200–300 mL/hour over 1–2 days."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Electrolyte Management Potassium Repletion : Necessary and guided by serum potassium measurements. (Option C ruled out) Consider Diuretics : Patients on diuretics may have significant potassium and magnesium deficits. Address Hypophosphatemia : Can occur during therapy; treated with potassium phosphate (KPO4) and nutritional support."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Insulin Therapy Initial Insulin Regimen : Start with an IV insulin bolus of 0.1 unit/kg , followed by a continuous infusion at 0.1 unit/kg/hour . (Option C ruled out) If serum glucose does not decrease, double the insulin infusion rate. Glucose Monitoring : When plasma glucose falls to 11.1-13.9 mmol/L (200–250 mg/dL) , add glucose to IV fluids and decrease insulin infusion to 0.02-0.1 unit/kg/hour. Continuation of Therapy : Insulin infusion should continue until the patient resumes oral intake and can transition to subcutaneous insulin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laboratory Abnormalities and Diagnosis of HHS"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Post-Hospitalization : Patients should be discharged on insulin, with some able to transition to oral glucose-lowering agents (Option D) later."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment Protocol in practice:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1) IV Fluids:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"0.9% Normal saline (1-3 L over 3 hrs) fl"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"0.45% Normal Saline (200-300 ml/hr)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"When blood glucose is around 250 mg/hr"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Add 5% dextrose n"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2) Insulin:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Short-acting insulin IV STAT"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Infusion 0.1 U/kg/hr"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administer IV Ringer Lactate (Option A): While IV Ringer Lactate is a common fluid for resuscitation, it contains potassium and calcium, which are not ideal for the initial management of HHS. The primary issue in HHS is dehydration, and normal saline (0.9% NaCl) is the preferred fluid for initial resuscitation."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 70-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes is admitted for confusion and severe dehydration. Her blood glucose is 900 mg/dL, and serum osmolality is elevated. Arterial blood gas reveals a normal pH and bicarbonate. Which of the following clinical signs would NOT be expected in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Hypotension"},{"t":"Tachycardia"},{"t":"Kussmaul breathing"},{"t":"Fever"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Kussmaul breathing"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Here, the patient has a Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar State (HHS) , which is associated with severe hyperglycemia and elevated serum osmolality but lacks significant acidosis . Kussmaul breathing is typically seen in patients with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) , not HHS. This is because Kussmaul breathing occurs as a compensatory mechanism for metabolic acidosis , where the body attempts to \"blow off\" carbon dioxide (COn) to reduce acidity in the blood."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar State (HHS) :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Typically seen in elderly individuals with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Symptoms : Characterized by a several-week history of: Polyuria Weight loss Diminished oral intake Acute Presentation : May culminate in: Mental confusion Lethargy Coma Physical Exam Findings : Profound dehydration and hyperosmolality Hypotension and tachycardia (Option A & B) Altered mental status Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and Kussmaul respirations (Option C) are absent (typical in diabetic ketoacidosis)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Precipitating Factors"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myocardial infarction Stroke Sepsis and pneumonia Other serious infections (Option D) Prior debilitating conditions (e.g., stroke, dementia) or social situations leading to compromised water intake."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Underlying Causes : Relative insulin deficiency and inadequate fluid intake . Mechanisms : Insulin deficiency leads to: Increased hepatic glucose production ( glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis ) Impaired glucose utilization in skeletal muscle. Resulting hyperglycemia causes osmotic diuresis, leading to intravascular volume depletion, worsened by inadequate fluid replacement. Absence of Ketosis possibly due to: Less severe insulin deficiency compared to DKA. Lower levels of counterregulatory hormones and free fatty acids. Reduced liver capability for ketone synthesis or an insulin/glucagon ratio unfavourable for ketogenesis."}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 28-year-old female with type 1 diabetes presents to the emergency department with increased thirst, frequent urination, nausea, abdominal pain, and signs of dehydration, including dry mucous membranes, tachycardia, and hypotension (BP: 98/60 mmHg). On examination, her vital signs include a heart rate of 110 bpm, respiratory rate of 22 breaths/min, and a blood pressure of 98/60 mmHg. Laboratory results show a blood glucose level of 350 mg/dL, serum bicarbonate of 18 mEq/L, and a pH of 7.30. What is the next step in management based on the ISPAD 2022 guidelines?","o":[{"t":"Start intravenous fluids and initiate insulin therapy"},{"t":"Administer subcutaneous insulin and monitor glucose"},{"t":"Provide oral hydration and adjust the diet accordingly"},{"t":"Ensure airway patency and consider nasogastric tube insertion"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Start intravenous fluids and initiate insulin therapy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: Clinical presentation of increased thirst, frequent urination, nausea, and abdominal pain, along with lab findings of blood glucose 350 mg/dL, serum bicarbonate 18 mEq/L, and pH 7.30, indicate Mild diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) with moderate dehydration. According to the ISPAD 2022 guidelines , in patients presenting with signs of moderate dehydration in DKA, the next step is to start intravenous fluids and initiate insulin therapy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Updated Management of Diabetic Ketoacidosis (ISPAD 2022):"},{"k":"img","v":5268},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Contraindication of Insulin Drip:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin should be withheld if the serum potassium level is below 3.3 mmol/L."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The target is to maintain serum potassium levels above 3.5 mmol/L (3.5 meq/L) to start the insulin drip."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemia can lead to diaphragmatic paralysis and potentially result in respiratory arrest."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administer subcutaneous insulin and monitor glucose (Option B): Subcutaneous insulin is inappropriate in this case due to the patient's moderate dehydration and the need for intravenous fluids and insulin therapy to effectively manage the acute phase of DKA."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Provide oral hydration and adjust the diet accordingly (Option C): Oral hydration is insufficient given the patient's signs of moderate dehydration and potential DKA; intravenous fluids are necessary for proper rehydration and stabilization."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ensure airway patency and consider nasogastric tube insertion (Option D): While airway management may be important in severe cases, it is not the immediate priority here, as the patient’s vital signs and consciousness level do not indicate airway compromise."}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 28-year-old male presents with tachycardia, hypotension, and drowsiness. He has a sweet smell of acetone on his breath. Laboratory results show a random blood sugar of 400 mg/dL, serum bicarbonate level of 12 mEq/L, arterial pH of 7.1, and serum b-hydroxybutyrate level of 6 mmol/L. What diagnosis is most consistent with this patient's condition?","o":[{"t":"Mild Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Moderate Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Severe Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar State"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Moderate Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical feature of drowsiness, along with the laboratory findings of a serum bicarbonate level of 12 mEq/L, an arterial pH of 7.1, and a serum b-hydroxybutyrate level of 6 mmol/L, are consistent with a diagnosis of Moderate Diabetic Ketoacidosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The severity of DKA:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Severity Mild DKA (Option A) Moderate DKA (Option B) Severe DKA (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Serum ß-hydroxybutyrate 3-4 4-8 >8 (mmol/L)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Arterial pH 7.25-7.3 7.0-7.24 <7.0"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Serum Bicarbonate 15-18 10-15 <10 (mEq/L)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anion Gap >10 >12 >12"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mental Status Alert Alert/drowsy Stupor/coma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Examination of DKA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sickly-sweet smell of acetone in breath."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart rate: Tachycardia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood pressure: Hypotension (postural or supine)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory rate: Tachypnea (both depth and rate) Kussmaul breathing: Characterized by tachypnea and increased depth of breath, is a classic sign of DKA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kussmaul breathing: Characterized by tachypnea and increased depth of breath, is a classic sign of DKA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kussmaul breathing: Characterized by tachypnea and increased depth of breath, is a classic sign of DKA."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations of DKA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urine ketostix: Detects acetoacetate and acetate, but cannot detect ß-hydroxybutyrate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum ß-hydroxybutyrate: Produced three times more than acetoacetate and is preferred for accurate diagnosis of DKA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Random Blood Sugar: 250 - 600 mg/dL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum electrolytes: Potassium: Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting. Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done. Sodium: Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose. Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Potassium: Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting. Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sodium: Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose. Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Potassium: Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting. Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sodium: Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose. Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total body potassium deficiency of 3-6 mEq/kg in DKA occurs due to osmotic diuresis and vomiting."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly, lowering serum levels, and potassium correction with IV potassium chloride (KCl) should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum sodium is reduced due to hyperglycemia: 1.6 meq/L reduction in sodium per 100 mg/dL rise in serum glucose."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dilutional hyponatremia is common due to water driven into the intravascular space from inside cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal function test: Elevated blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis: pH: 6.8-7.3 pCO2: 20-30 mmHg HCO3: <18 meq/L Anion gap: Increased Indicates DKA causes High anion gap metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pH: 6.8-7.3"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pCO2: 20-30 mmHg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HCO3: <18 meq/L"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anion gap: Increased"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indicates DKA causes High anion gap metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum amylase and lipase: High"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total leukocyte count: Increased"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phosphorus and magnesium levels: Reduced, but not accurately shown due to hypovolemia and hyperglycemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pH: 6.8-7.3"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pCO2: 20-30 mmHg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HCO3: <18 meq/L"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anion gap: Increased"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indicates DKA causes High anion gap metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar State (Option D) (HHS) typically presents with extremely high blood glucose levels (often >600 mg/dL), significant dehydration, and a high serum osmolality, but without significant ketoacidosis. The patient's blood glucose of 400 mg/dL and the presence of ketosis (evidenced by the sweet smell of acetone and elevated b-hydroxybutyrate) make HHS unlikely"}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 40-year-old male with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes presents with vomiting and shortness of breath. He appears dehydrated and tachycardic, with a fruity odour on his breath. His lab results show elevated blood glucose and ketones. Which of the following is not a risk factor for his condition?","o":[{"t":"Infection"},{"t":"Pancreatitis"},{"t":"Hypothyroidism"},{"t":"Inadequate insulin administration"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Hypothyroidism"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The clinical presentation with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes, vomiting, shortness of breath, dehydration, tachycardia, and fruity breath odour, along with elevated blood glucose and ketones, is most consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). Hyperthyroidism, not hypothyroidism, can precipitate DKA in diabetes through increased intestinal glucose absorption, decreased insulin secretion and half-life, and reduced peripheral glucose utilization due to insulin resistance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Precipitating Factors:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inadequate insulin administration (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infection (pneumonia/UTI/gastroenteritis/sepsis) (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infarction (cerebral, coronary, mesenteric, peripheral)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatitis (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Drugs (cocaine)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pregnancy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology:"},{"k":"img","v":5242},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptoms Signs"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nausea, vomiting Tachycardia Abdominal pain Dehydration Shortness of breath Hypotension (postural or supine) Thirst, polyuria Tachypnea Weight loss Kussmaul respirations (air hunger) Weakness Smell of acetone Leg cramps Respiratory distress Blurred vision Hypothermia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cold extremities/peripheral cyanosis Abdominal tenderness (may resemble acute pancreatitis or surgical abdomen) Lethargy/obtundation/cerebral oedema/possibly coma"}]}]);
